  const INSIGHTS            = {
  "Q1": "<p>At <strong>22.3% (5,288 posts)</strong>, Selling on Amazon is the dominant topic, reflecting the core business focus of the community. However, sample sentiment reveals deep dissatisfaction: sellers are openly discussing <strong>switching to affiliate marketing</strong> due to perceived fee structures and platform policies they view as unfair. One post notes 'seller thì ít bỏ hết sang làm aff rồi' (sellers are abandoning the platform for affiliate work), signaling potential churn risk. The frustration centers on <strong>cumulative fee burden</strong> and perceived lack of seller agency—some even advocate boycotts as the only leverage point.</p>",
  "Q2": "<p>Other-platform sellers (Shopee, Lazada, TikTok Shop, etc.) account for <strong>13,146 of 13,930 mentions (94.3%)</strong>, while Amazon sellers contribute only <strong>1,428 (10.2%)</strong>. This reflects the Vietnamese e-commerce ecosystem's current seller base: most are entrenched on domestic platforms. Sample posts reveal frustration with platform fees and affiliate costs, suggesting sellers are exploring alternatives but not yet migrating to Amazon at scale. The low Amazon seller engagement indicates either low awareness, high barriers to entry, or preference for established local platforms.</p>",
  "Q3": "<p>Account creation represents <strong>26.5% of prospect discussions</strong> vs only <strong>4.7% for sellers</strong>—a massive 21.8-point gap. Within this topic, <strong>Account Verification (9.9% of prospects)</strong> and <strong>Identity Verification/SIV (6.52%)</strong> dominate. Prospects are wrestling with documentation, legal entity setup, and account type selection before they can even list. This is a critical onboarding friction point that sellers have long since resolved.</p>",
  "Q4": "<p>Community sentiment reveals <strong>significant seller defection toward affiliate/KOL channels</strong> due to compounding fee structures on Amazon Vietnam. One user explicitly states sellers are \"abandoning the platform for affiliate work,\" citing unsustainable cost-of-goods-sold when combining platform fees, affiliate commissions, and KOL incentives. This signals <strong>margin compression at the seller tier</strong> and potential GMV leakage to off-platform channels. The frustration centers on fee opacity and lack of negotiating power against a well-resourced legal/commercial team.</p>",
  "Q5": "<p>Cost & Fees is the dominant negative topic with <strong>991 mentions (21% of all negative sentiment)</strong>, driven by sellers' acute frustration over cumulative platform charges. Sample posts reveal sellers calculating <strong>30%+ platform fees + 10% advertising costs + taxes</strong>, totaling <strong>40% margin loss per order</strong>. One seller explicitly states: \"Cứ 100tr sàn thu 40tr\" (For every 100M revenue, the platform takes 40M). This is not a pricing complaint—it's a profitability crisis that makes sellers question whether to continue selling at all. The compounding fee structure (commission + ads + potential policy penalties) is eroding trust and viability.</p>",
  "Q6": "<p>Sellers are experiencing <strong>cumulative fee pressure exceeding 40% per transaction</strong> when combining platform fees (30%+), advertising costs (10%), and taxes. Multiple posts reveal sellers operating at break-even or loss, with one explicitly stating \"mỗi đơn mất cho sàn tầm 40% chưa tính thuế\" and another declaring readiness to quit: \"sập đi tôi tình nguyện nghỉ bán trước.\" This is not a pricing complaint—it's a <strong>business viability crisis</strong> that threatens seller retention.</p>",
  "Q7": "<p>With <strong>735 mentions of success & income</strong> against only 122 for passive income and 95 for ease of entry, sellers are joining Amazon primarily for <strong>immediate revenue generation</strong>, not long-term passive streams. Sample posts reveal sellers chasing viral trends, seasonal campaigns (graduation stoles, banners), and commission-based roles—all framed as quick-win opportunities. This suggests Amazon's value proposition must emphasize <strong>proven earning pathways and realistic income timelines</strong>, not vague 'passive' promises that contradict the data.</p>",
  "Q8": "<p>Nine sellers explicitly cite <strong>scam concerns</strong> as their reason for leaving Amazon Vietnam. Posts reveal a pattern: sellers are being <strong>defrauded by fake buyer addresses</strong> (e.g., \"multiple accounts buying same product, shipping to one address\") and <strong>warned by peers about fraudulent schemes</strong>. The community's response is cynical—\"you got scammed,\" \"don't ignore it\"—suggesting Amazon's anti-fraud communication is either absent or ineffective. This erodes trust faster than any policy change.</p>",
  "Q9": "<p>The data reveals a stark asymmetry: <strong>Sellers (Others) account for 837 join mentions and 454 leave mentions</strong>, while <strong>Amazon sellers contribute only 87 and 34 respectively</strong>. This 9.6× disparity in join discussions and 13.4× in leave discussions suggests the Vietnamese e-commerce community is primarily discussing alternative platforms (Shopee, Lazada, TikTok Shop, etc.). Amazon's minimal presence in churn conversations indicates either low adoption or low engagement—sellers are not actively debating leaving Amazon because they were never heavily invested to begin with.</p>",
  "Q10": "<p><strong>Apparel & Fashion (306 mentions) and Jewelry & Accessories (211 mentions)</strong> together account for 54% of category discussions. These low-barrier-to-entry categories attract high seller interest, but community posts reveal growing frustration with <strong>unsustainable fee structures and affiliate commission stacking</strong>. Sellers are increasingly pivoting to affiliate models rather than direct sales, driven by rising platform costs and competitive pressure.</p>",
  "Q11": "<p><strong>FBA is the most-used Amazon tool (118 mentions)</strong>, but only <strong>23% of users report satisfaction (27/118)</strong>. This 77% dissatisfaction gap signals systemic issues beyond user preference. Combined with <strong>5 reported FBA issues</strong> and the broader finding that <strong>system errors/bugs account for 58% of all tool complaints (36/62)</strong>, FBA appears to suffer from reliability and stability problems that erode trust despite widespread adoption.</p>",
  "Q12": "<p>Accounting and tax services represent a critical pain point for SOA sellers, with <strong>110 mentions and only 62% satisfaction</strong>—the lowest among all major third-party services. This 32-point gap versus shipping services (94% satisfaction) signals widespread frustration with current accounting solutions. Sellers face complex Vietnamese tax compliance, multi-platform reporting, and FBA inventory accounting challenges that existing providers are failing to address adequately. This service category demands immediate attention as it directly impacts seller profitability and regulatory compliance.</p>",
  "Q13": "<p><strong>General training dominates with 17 mentions</strong>, yet sellers are actively requesting concrete course links and schedules. The samples reveal sellers asking \"Chờ link khóa học\" and requesting specific coaching contacts, indicating <strong>demand exists but course information is scattered or hard to find</strong>. Amazon should consolidate training offerings into a centralized, easy-to-access resource with clear enrollment pathways.</p>",
  "Q14": "<p>With <strong>46 mentions (30% of growth topics)</strong>, team building dominates seller priorities. However, the <strong>29 mixed-sentiment posts</strong> reveal a critical gap: sellers know they need teams (marketing, R&D, operations) but struggle with hiring, delegation, and cost control. Sample posts show sellers posting freelance R&D roles with productivity-based pay and seeking marketing outsourcing advice, indicating they're experimenting with lean, flexible team models rather than committing to full-time hires. This reflects both opportunity and friction in scaling operations.</p>"
};
